    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class (the others are a base and a helper). The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented idempotent owner creation for `POST /api/owners`: a repeat with an already-seen `Idempotency-Key` header now replays the originally created owner with **200** instead of creating a duplicate.\n\n**How it works** \u2014 two small, self-contained function steps bracket the existing pipeline:\n\n- **`CheckIdempotencyKey`** (new entry-point step): reads the `Idempotency-Key` header via `ServerHttpConnection` (null when absent). If the key maps to a previously created owner, it responds `200` with that owner and stops; otherwise it takes the `proceed` flow into the existing `build` step. Unknown/absent keys flow straight through unchanged.\n- **`RecordIdempotencyKey`** (new step after `save`): once the new owner has an id, records `key \u2192 id` so a later repeat replays it.\n\n**Files touched (3):**\n- `CheckIdempotencyKey.java` (new) \u2014 holds the shared `SEEN` map and the package-private `key(connection)` header helper (kept non-public so the class still has exactly one public method).\n- `RecordIdempotencyKey.java` (new) \u2014 one tiny method reusing that helper.\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 added the `checkIdempotent` entry step (with `outputs: { proceed: build }`) and the `record` step between `save` and `audit`.\n\n**Keeping structural impact low:** no existing function was modified \u2014 the behaviour is added as two cohesive new classes, each with near-zero surrounding complexity (WMC_other \u2248 0), so no large method or class grew. The only edit to existing code is the YAML wiring.\n\nBoth the new `AcceptanceTest.coreIdempotentRepeat` and the whole suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\nOne design note worth flagging: the seen-key store is a process-wide in-memory map. That satisfies the spec and the test, but it isn't shared across instances or bounded/evicted \u2014 if you later want durable or multi-node idempotency, that store would need to move to the database or a cache.",
